          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between E11 and E11.9, and when is each the right thing to quote?",
          "answer": "**The short answer:** E11 is the category, the broad heading for type 2 diabetes. E11.9 is a more specific subcode. The retrieved material confirms the category's role, but it does not fully describe E11.9 or the rule for choosing between them. I could not retrieve that rule, so I won't state it.\n\n**What the sources support:**\n- Type 2 diabetes is category E11 in ICD-10-CM, in Chapter 4 (endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases). The chapter splits diabetes by cause, not by treatment. Type 1, type 2 and other types each have their own category. (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*; *ICD-10-CM Chapter 4*)\n- If a record says only \"diabetes mellitus\" with no type, the classification defaults to type 2. (*Coding type 2 diabetes*)\n- Taking insulin does not change the category. A person with type 2 diabetes on insulin is still coded to E11, and insulin use is recorded as a separate status code. (*Coding type 2 diabetes*)\n- The E11 document is titled \"E11 and its complication subcategories,\" which suggests E11 has further subcodes for complications. The passages do not describe E11.9 itself or explain when it applies.\n- A related principle appears in the code-structure guidance: where a combination code exists, it is used instead of separate codes. The passages do not say this applies to E11.9 specifically. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n\n**What I could not confirm:** the convention that a more specific code is preferred when the record supports it. My search for that passage ran out of budget, so I can't quote it. A coder or the official ICD-10-CM guidelines would be the right place to check when E11.9 versus a more specific code should be used.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
